{
  "thought_process": "This is round 1 with no history. Current params: P=0.75, I=0.005, D=0.0. Setpoint ~41.47. Analyzing the time series: the system starts near setpoint (~42.89), then the input climbs to a peak of ~54.0 at t=305820, which is about 12.5 units above setpoint (30.2% overshoot confirmed). After the peak, the system oscillates back down, passing through setpoint and going below (undershoot visible as negative errors reaching -9.18). The system is clearly oscillating with 2 zero-crossings. The PWM output peaks at about 8.1 and troughs at -6.9, showing the controller is driving the system hard in both directions. The oscillation amplitude is large and sustained, indicating the current P=0.75 is already causing significant overshoot and oscillation. The I=0.005 is very small and unlikely contributing much to the oscillation. The root problem: P=0.75 is too high for this system, causing 30% overshoot and sustained oscillation. We are still in Phase 1 (P tuning). Strategy: We need to REDUCE P significantly to damp the oscillation. The per-round guardrail says keep P within ~3x current value, so max P would be 2.25 and min would be 0.25. Since P is causing oscillation, we should reduce P. A 50% reduction to P=0.37 or 0.40 would be appropriate. I should also keep I very small to avoid adding to instability. Actually, with 30% overshoot and clear oscillation, I should reduce P substantially. Let me go to P=0.40 (about 53% of current). Keep I at 0.005 (very small, negligible effect). D=0.0. This conservative reduction should reduce the oscillation amplitude significantly while maintaining some control authority. The system appears to have a relatively fast natural response given the 110ms sampling interval and the oscillation completing roughly a half-cycle in about 2.4 seconds. Reducing P from 0.75 to 0.40 is a meaningful but safe reduction within guardrail limits.",
  "analysis_summary": "First round. Current P=0.75 produces 30.2% overshoot and sustained oscillation (2 zero-crossings). Input peaks at ~54.0 (setpoint ~41.5, overshoot ~12.5 units). PWM swings from +8.1 to -6.9, indicating aggressive control action. The system is clearly over-gained. I=0.005 is negligible. Phase 1 (P tuning) applies: P must be reduced to damp the oscillation and bring overshoot below 5%. No history to compare against.",
  "tuning_action": "Reduce P from 0.75 to 0.40 (~53% of current) to significantly reduce overshoot and damp oscillation. Keep I=0.005 unchanged (negligible contribution). Keep D=0.0. This stays well within the 3x guardrail (range 0.25-2.25) and is a conservative reduction appropriate for hardware safety. Observe next round for reduced overshoot and oscillation count.",
  "p": 0.40,
  "i": 0.005,
  "d": 0.0,
  "status": "TUNING"
}

